<article class="env-lemma" id="06S1">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06S1">Lemma <span data-tag="06S1">72.10.9</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a monomorphism of algebraic spaces over $k$. If $Y$ is locally quasi-finite over $k$ so is $X$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $Y$ is locally quasi-finite over $k$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/06S0" data-tag="06S0">72.10.8</a> we see that $Y = \coprod \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A_ i)$ where each $A_ i$ is an Artinian local ring finite over $k$. By Decent Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/06RZ" data-tag="06RZ">68.19.1</a> we see that $X$ is a scheme. Consider $X_ i = f^{-1}(\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A_ i))$. Then $X_ i$ has either one or zero points. If $X_ i$ has zero points there is nothing to prove. If $X_ i$ has one point, then $X_ i = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B_ i)$ with $B_ i$ a zero dimensional local ring and $A_ i \to B_ i$ is an epimorphism of rings. In particular $A_ i/\mathfrak m_{A_ i} = B_ i/\mathfrak m_{A_ i}B_ i$ and we see that $A_ i \to B_ i$ is surjective by Nakayama's lemma, Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00DV" data-tag="00DV">10.20.1</a> (because $\mathfrak m_{A_ i}$ is a nilpotent ideal!). Thus $B_ i$ is a finite local $k$-algebra, and we conclude by Lemma <a href="/tag/06S0" data-tag="06S0">72.10.8</a> that $X \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k)$ is locally quasi-finite. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>